cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use these materials, please cite them as below.
type: dataset
title: 'Build, Break, and Question a PartyRock Phishing Detector'
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-09-19
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: https://github.com/DTC-Consortium/DTC-Consortium-MLU
url: https://github.com/DTC-Consortium/DTC-Consortium-MLU/tree/main/mlu/course-elements/mlu-000007-partyrock-phishing-detector
abstract: >-
  Students build a phishing-email classifier in Amazon PartyRock, classify at least 15
  emails themselves before the AI sees them, then compare both against instructor-held
  ground truth. The assignment then inverts: students spend a round trying to fool the
  detector they just built, log every technique, and write up where a confident-sounding
  verdict should not be trusted. No code, and the reflection is worth more than the build.
authors:
  - name: 'Sara Mathew'
    affiliation: 'Oklahoma City Community College'
keywords:
  - 'phishing detection'
  - 'adversarial testing'
  - 'prompt engineering'
  - 'human-in-the-loop'
  - 'AI confidence versus correctness'
  - 'cybersecurity literacy'
